# PSP ad hoc over nx-mobile's phone-to-phone USB cable as well as the Wi-Fi (on top of 0012): the runner decides which
# network the link is on and routes every socket the core hands it there. The game's PDP sockets and PTP listen
# sockets outlive a group, so when the runner asks for a new login (as it does on every move between networks) and
# after each login they are routed again onto the link's network; a socket left on the old one would send where the
# new peers are not. While a phone is on the port and its cable is coming up, the runner holds its answer for the
# server up to NX_PSP_LINK_SERVER_WAIT_MAX_MS (nx_psp_link.h); a game's Scan / Create / Connect / Join waits for the
# login that long, and for the connect after it, since a request that returns BUSY is a failure to some games
# (Tekken 6 calls Connect on entering its lobby). Leaving ad hoc tells the runner before the login thread is joined, so
# a held login gives up at once rather than stall the game. Without a frontend that answers the call, the core behaves
# as upstream.
diff --git a/Core/HLE/NxPspLink.cpp b/Core/HLE/NxPspLink.cpp
index 04287d2..2f4c90e 100644
--- a/Core/HLE/NxPspLink.cpp
+++ b/Core/HLE/NxPspLink.cpp
@@ -304,6 +304,7 @@ bool InAdhoc() {
 
 void RouteSocket(int fd) {
 	if (enabled && iface.route_socket) {
+		// The frontend chooses the network (the Wi-Fi or the cable) from where the link is; the argument is unused.
 		iface.route_socket(fd, 0);
 	}
 }
